    "description": "The Chegem Gorge is one of the most popular among tourists and beautiful places in Kabardino-Balkaria. The gorge attracts with its waterfalls, beautiful and narrow gorges, in some places where the mountains are so close together that it can be difficult for two cars to pass. The gorge is located 40 minutes drive from the capital of Kabardino-Balkaria - the city of Nalchik.\r\n\r\n[gallery ids=\"3550,3549,3375\"]\r\n\r\nIn the Chegem Gorge, you can have a good rest and recharge with a lot of positive emotions, see unique landscapes, and enjoy the atmosphere of an off-road route. Here you can see and feel the traditions of the locals, admire the untouched nature, and paragliding.\r\nTour Program:\n\n\r\n[timeline]\r\n[timeline_item item_number=\"1\" title=\"Chegem waterfalls\"] First stop - Chegem waterfalls. The largest of them is the \"Maiden's Spit\". According to legend, girl with luxurious, long hair lived in the village. Robbers attacked them, but the girl did not surrender, but jumped off the cliff, catching her hair on the stones. This is how the waterfall was formed. The second waterfall looks like a gutter, through which streams of water flow without ceasing. The third waterfall is \"weeping\". Thin streams of water pour out through cracks in the rocks, resembling tears.\r\n\r\n[gallery ids=\"3539,3535,3537\"]\r\n\r\nThe panorama is breathtaking and excites the imagination. There is a lot of vegetation around, birds sing, insects chirp, and all this is combined with the incessant sound of water.\r\n[/timeline_item]\r\n\r\n[timeline_item item_number=\"2\" title=\"El Tu-Bue Village\"]\r\n\r\nThen we go to the village of El Tu-Byu, where we will walk around the city of the Dead.\r\n\r\nThe village is very interesting, once the national poet of the republic Kaysin Kuliev lived here. A monument was erected to him in the center of the village. The village itself is like an open-air museum, where you can see the watchtowers of the ancient highlanders.\r\n\r\n[gallery ids=\"3566,3559,3540\"]\r\n\r\nThe \"city of the dead\" is bordered by a small stone wall. It has preserved 8 ground keshene (mausoleums). Half of which have a triangular roof, and the other half with a domed roof. Here, fenced with low stone fences, there are family graves. But they don't have any markings. Being here, willy-nilly, you feel awe. Some of the crypts were destroyed and looted. You can look inside through the windows. Whether there were burials there and where they then disappeared remains a mystery. After all, the windows are very small and i",
